\title{A Divisor Bridge Between the P\'olya Tree Functional Equation and Its Coefficient Recurrence}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 25, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $(a_k)_{k\ge 1}$ be the enumeration sequence of rooted unlabelled trees (P\'olya
trees; OEIS A000081), with ordinary generating function $A(z)=\sum_{k\ge 1} a_k z^k$
satisfying $A(z)=z\,\ex(A(z))\,\Phi(z)$, where $\Phi(z)=\ex\!\big(\sum_{i\ge 2} A(z^i)/i\big)$.
We isolate the arithmetic core of the classical passage from this functional equation to
the practical coefficient recurrence. Writing $S(z)=\sum_{i\ge 1} A(z^i)/i$ so that
$A=z\ex(S)$, the logarithmic derivative gives the exponential-free identity
$zA'(z)=A(z)\,(1+zS'(z))$. We prove a single \emph{divisor bridge} identity,
$n\cdot[z^n]S(z)=\omega_n$ with $\omega_n=\sum_{d\mid n} d\,a_d$, the only non-formal
ingredient connecting the analytic series $S$ to the number-theoretic divisor weight
$\omega$. From it we derive, and establish the logical \emph{equivalence} with, the
recurrence $a_k=\frac{1}{k-1}\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} a_j\,\omega_{k-j}$ for $k\ge 2$, with
$a_1=1$. All results hold for an arbitrary coefficient sequence over $\QQ$, so the bridge
is a property of the algebra rather than of the specific tree counts, and have been
formally verified.
\end{abstract}

