\section{Introduction}

The enumeration of rooted unlabelled trees goes back to Cayley and was given its
definitive symmetry-aware treatment by P\'olya. The counts
\[
1,1,2,4,9,20,48,115,286,719,1842,4766,\dots
\]
form OEIS A000081 and arise throughout chemistry (alkane isomers), computer science (the
shapes of hierarchical data), and phylogenetics. The generating function $A(z)$ obeys a
functional equation encoding the ``root above a multiset of subtrees'' description,
corrected for the indistinguishability of isomorphic branches:
\begin{equation}\tag{FE}
A(z)=z\,\ex\!\big(A(z)\big)\,\Phi(z), \qquad \Phi(z)=\ex\!\left(\sum_{i\ge 2}\frac{A(z^i)}{i}\right).
\end{equation}
In practice one computes with a recurrence rather than with (FE). This paper exposes,
with full rigor, \emph{why} the recurrence has its particular form, and pinpoints where
arithmetic (the divisor structure) enters what is otherwise pure power-series
bookkeeping. A single identity --- the \emph{divisor bridge} --- does all the work, and
the functional equation (in log-derivative form) and the recurrence turn out to be
logically equivalent for any candidate coefficient sequence.

We work over $\QQ$ with sequences $a\colon\NN\to\QQ$ and treat all manipulations formally
(coefficientwise), so no convergence is required. The results have been formally
verified; the proof sketches below are faithful to that development.

\section{Definitions}

We write $\sum_{d\mid n}$ for the sum over positive divisors of $n$.

\begin{definition}[P\'olya tree generating function]
$A(z)=\sum_{k\ge 1} a_k z^k$ is the ordinary generating function of $(a_k)$; set $a_0=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Divisor weight $\omega$, \texttt{omegaSeq}]
For a sequence $a$ and $n\in\NN$,
\[
\omega_n=\sum_{d\mid n} d\cdot a_d.
\]
This is the Euler-transform logarithmic-derivative weight; e.g.\ $\omega_6=a_1+2a_2+3a_3+6a_6$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Coefficients of $S$, \texttt{sCoeff}]
Let $S(z)=\sum_{i\ge 1} A(z^i)/i$. Its $n$-th coefficient is
\[
s_n=[z^n]\,S(z)=\sum_{i\mid n}\frac{a_{n/i}}{i}.
\]
Indeed $S(z)=\sum_{i\ge1}\frac1i\sum_{k\ge1} a_k z^{ik}$, and grouping by $n=ik$ gives the
divisor sum.
\end{definition}

The series $S$ is the natural intermediary: (FE) is exactly $A=z\ex(S)$, since
$\ex(A(z))\,\Phi(z)=\ex\!\big(\sum_{i\ge1} A(z^i)/i\big)=\ex(S(z))$.

\section{The log-derivative reformulation}

Since $A=z\ex(S)$ with $a_1\ne0$, $A$ is $z$ times a unit, so $\log A=\log z+S$ is well
defined formally. Differentiating, $A'/A=1/z+S'$, and multiplying by $zA$ yields the
exponential-free identity
\begin{equation}\tag{LD}
z\,A'(z)=A(z)\,\big(1+z\,S'(z)\big).
\end{equation}
For sequences with $a_1\ne0$, (LD) is equivalent to (FE), but (LD) is a polynomial
identity amenable to direct coefficient extraction.

\section{The divisor bridge}

\begin{theorem}[Divisor bridge, \texttt{divisor\_bridge}]\label{thm:db}
For every sequence $a\colon\NN\to\QQ$ and every $n$,
\[
n\cdot s_n=\omega_n,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad n\sum_{i\mid n}\frac{a_{n/i}}{i}=\sum_{d\mid n} d\,a_d.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write the left side as $\sum_{i\mid n}\frac{n}{i}a_{n/i}$. The divisor reflection
$i\mapsto n/i$ is a bijection on the divisors of $n$; substituting $d=n/i$ uses
$\frac{n}{i}\cdot i=n$ for $i\mid n$ (so the rational cast $(n/i:\QQ)=n/i$ is valid). The
term indexed by $d$ becomes $d\cdot a_d$, and the two divisor sums agree term by term.
\end{proof}

Since differentiation multiplies the $n$-th coefficient by $n$, Theorem~\ref{thm:db} reads
\begin{equation}\tag{DB}
[z^n]\,\big(z\,S'(z)\big)=n\,s_n=\omega_n.
\end{equation}
This is the only non-formal step; everything downstream is Cauchy-product algebra.

\section{From the log-derivative identity to the recurrence}

\begin{lemma}[Convolution rewrite, \texttt{feSum\_eq\_omegaSum}]\label{lem:conv}
For every $n$,
\[
\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} a_j\big((n-j)\,s_{n-j}\big)=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} a_j\,\omega_{n-j}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:db} with argument $n-j$ to each summand.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bridge equivalence, \texttt{polya\_FE\_iff\_recurrence}]\label{thm:equiv}
For every sequence $a\colon\NN\to\QQ$, the coefficientwise log-derivative identity
\begin{equation}\tag{LD$_n$}
\forall n\ge1:\quad n\,a_n=a_n+\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} a_j\big((n-j)\,s_{n-j}\big)
\end{equation}
is equivalent to the recurrence
\begin{equation}\tag{R$_k$}
\forall k\ge2:\quad (k-1)\,a_k=\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} a_j\,\omega_{k-j}.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(LD$_n$) is exactly $[z^n]$ of $zA'=A(1+zS')$: the left is $na_n$; on the right $A\cdot1$
gives $a_n$ and $A\cdot zS'$ gives $\sum_j a_j[z^{n-j}](zS')$, with the $j=n$ term zero
since $[z^0](zS')=0$. ($\Rightarrow$) From (LD$_k$), Lemma~\ref{lem:conv} converts the
convolution to $\sum_j a_j\omega_{k-j}$; moving $a_k$ left gives $na_k-a_k=(k-1)a_k$, i.e.\
(R$_k$). ($\Leftarrow$) For $n=1$, (LD$_1$) is $a_1=a_1+0$, trivially true; for $n\ge2$,
reverse the computation using (R$_n$) and Lemma~\ref{lem:conv}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[P\'olya tree recurrence, main result, \texttt{polya\_tree\_recurrence}]\label{thm:main}
Suppose $a_1=1$ and (LD$_n$) holds for all $n\ge1$. Then $a_1=1$ and for every $k\ge2$,
\[
a_k=\frac{1}{k-1}\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} a_j\,\omega_{k-j},\qquad \omega_m=\sum_{d\mid m} d\,a_d.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv}, (LD$_n$) for all $n$ gives (R$_k$) for $k\ge2$. Since
$k-1\ne0$ in $\QQ$, divide.
\end{proof}

Because $k-1$ is invertible in any commutative $\QQ$-algebra $R$, the proofs of
Theorems~\ref{thm:equiv} and~\ref{thm:main} hold verbatim over $R$ (e.g.\ $\RR$, $\CC$,
$\QQ[[t]]$); no ordering or archimedean input is used.

\section{Algorithm}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is directly executable. Given a bound $N$: set $a_0=0$, $a_1=1$;
for $k=2,\dots,N$ compute the weights $\omega_m=\sum_{d\mid m} d\,a_d$ ($m\le k-1$), then
$a_k=\frac{1}{k-1}\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} a_j\,\omega_{k-j}$. Maintaining all divisor weights up
to $N$ costs $O(N\log N)$ and the convolutions cost $O(N^2)$, so the sequence is produced
in $O(N^2)$ rational operations; with exact arithmetic each $a_k$ is an integer.

\section{Worked example}

With $a_1=1$, $\omega_1=1$: $a_2=\frac11(a_1\omega_1)=1$ ($\omega_2=3$);
$a_3=\frac12(3+1)=2$ ($\omega_3=7$); $a_4=\frac13(7+3+2)=4$ ($\omega_4=19$);
$a_5=\frac14(19+7+6+4)=9$. These reproduce A000081: $1,1,2,4,9,20,48,115,286,719,1842,4766$.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The construction shows $\omega$ is \emph{derived}, not assumed: it is the coefficient of
$zS'(z)$ via (DB), so Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is not the recurrence in disguise. Although
the recurrence divides by $k-1$ over $\QQ$, the outputs are integers, reflecting that
$a_k$ counts trees; a structural proof of integrality should follow from the
Euler-transform form. The weight $\omega_k=\sum_{d\mid k} d\,a_d$ is the universal
Euler-transform log-derivative weight, so for any species with generating function
$F=x\,G(F)\,\ex(\sum_{i\ge2}F(x^i)/i)$ only the outer $G$ changes; the divisor machinery
is identical. The recurrence is also the standard route to the growth law
$a_k\sim C\,\alpha^k k^{-3/2}$ with Otter's constant $\alpha\approx2.9557$.

Future directions: (1) prove integrality via the Euler transform; (2) restate over
arbitrary $\QQ$-algebras; (3) build a uniform Euler-transform family of recurrences;
(4) derive Otter asymptotics from the recurrence; (5) upgrade uniqueness to a certified
evaluator.

